d testbench with spec-derived reference model for mac8", "summary": "Verilog-2001 testbench for the mac8 block in [artifact:prj-de4daabcef.design-under-test.a1], serving [requirement:req-fdee493310]. The reference model (task model_step) is written clause by clause from [artifact:prj-de4daabcef.interface-spec.a1]. Priority is reset, then clear, then accumulate, then hold. It adds a 32-bit unsigned product, saturates when the total would exceed 0xFFFFFF, and keeps a sticky flag. The testbench drives inputs at the falling edge, updates the model at the rising edge and compares 1 ns later. Each cycle compare counts one pass or one fail, and the first 10 mismatches print the cycle, inputs, previous model state, DUT values and model values. Corner counters cover these events: saturation, exact limit without saturation, sticky hold, clr with en, clr while saturated, reset with en, and hold. Any counter left at zero adds a failure. At the end it prints one verdict line, 'NIRMAAN-SIM <nonce> <n> passed, <m> failed', and then calls $finish. The nonce is read from a plusarg; see claims for the assumed names.", "entry": "tb_mac8"}], "tool_runs": [], "claims": ["The nonce plusarg name is not stated in my inputs. The testbench tries +nonce=, +NIRMAAN_NONCE=, +nirmaan_nonce= and +NONCE= in that order, and counts a failure if none is present. If the platform uses another name, the verdict carries an empty nonce and the run fails; that would be a plusarg naming issue, not a design failure.", "I have not run this testbench, on the approved RTL or on any mutant. Whether it passes the clean RTL and catches planted mutants is for the platform's simulator.run and must-fail scorer to show.", "These mutants are the ones the scenarios are designed to catch (my reasoning, not shown): saturation removed or replaced by wrap; the limit compare changed from 'more than' to 'at least'; sat not sticky, or cleared on hold; clr losing to en; en ignored on hold; signed multiply; reset or clear leaving sat set."], "escalation": null, "notes": "Inputs used: the approved spec content [artifact:prj-de4daabcef.interface-spec.a1] (review record [evidence:prj-de4daabcef.interface-spec.e1]) and the approved RTL [artifact:prj-de4daabcef.design-under-test.a1] (review record [evidence:prj-de4daabcef.design-under-test.e1]). I read the RTL only for port names and widths; the model's behaviour comes from the spec text. The nonce is printed byte by byte, skipping NUL bytes, so leading padding in the packed string does not corrupt the verdict line under either Icarus or Verilator. Random stimulus comes from an in-file xorshift32 with a fixed seed (32'hC0FFEE01), so runs are repeatable and do not depend on simulator $random behaviour. The testbench has no watchdog, so that only one verdict line can ever be printed. The evidence requirements still open are the simulator.run on the approved RTL and an independent review record; neither is claimed here."}
